# DocPal V5 Project Rules & Conventions

## Project Overview
DocPal V5 is a modern, modular document management platform designed for enterprise workflows. Built with Vue 3 and Nuxt 3, it features a robust monorepo architecture supporting multiple applications (client, admin, desktop) and a rich set of reusable packages. DocPal V5 streamlines document creation, collaboration, sharing, and workflow automation, making it easy to manage complex document lifecycles securely and efficiently.

---

## Project Structure
- `apps/` — Deployable applications (client, admin, desktop)
- `packages/` — Shared code and components
- `pages/` — Shared pages used across apps
- `libraries/` — External libraries (API, PDF.js, etc.)

### i18n Locales
- `libraries/deployment/src/en-US.json`
- `libraries/deployment/src/zh-HK.json`
- `libraries/deployment/src/zh-CN.json`

### CSS Variables
- All CSS variables must be defined in `packages/base/assets/styles/main.scss`.
- Use the naming convention: `--app-variable-name`.
- Document new variables with comments for clarity.
- Example:
  ```scss
  // Primary brand color
  --app-primary-color: #409eff;
  ```

---

## Unit Testing
- Place unit test files in the same directory as the component file.
- Use [Vitest](mdc:https:/vitest.dev) as the testing framework.
- Mock all component-exposed functions and props as needed for isolation.
- Example test file: `MyComponent.spec.ts` next to `MyComponent.vue`.

---

## BRD (Business Requirement Document) Rule
- Place BRD files in the root `/brd` folder.
- Name files as `{gitUserName}-{YYYYMMDD}-{task-name}.md`.
- Example: `SeanTsang-20250602-generate-random-number.md`

---

## Task Management
- Place task files in the root `/tasks` folder.
- Name files as `{gitUserName}-{YYYYMMDD}-{task-name}.md`.
- Use numbered lists for tasks and subtasks. Subtasks should be prefixed with their parent task number.
- Always create a task list for each task and mark tasks as done when completed.
- Example:
  ```md
  [ ] 1 Main Task
    - [ ] 1.1 Subtask
    - [ ] 1.2 Subtask
  [ ] 2 Another Task
  ```

---

## General Rules
- You are a Senior Frontend Developer and an expert in Vue 3, Nuxt 3, JavaScript, TypeScript, HTML, and CSS.
- Always write correct, best practice, DRY, bug-free, fully functional, and working code.
- Prioritize easy-to-read code over performance.
- Use early returns for readability.
- Always use SCSS for styling.
- Always use `<script setup lang="ts">` for Vue components.
- Use descriptive variable and function names. Event handlers should be prefixed with `handle`, e.g., `handleClick`.
- Implement accessibility features (e.g., `tabindex="0"`, `aria-label`, `@click`, `@keydown`). Example:
  ```vue
  <a
    tabindex="0"
    aria-label="Open document"
    @click="handleClick"
    @keydown.enter="handleClick"
  >Open</a>
  ```
- Prefer function declarations over arrow function consts for top-level functions.
- Always use `v-tooltip` for tooltips when a tooltip is needed.
- Always review i18n option in page if any thing updated.
- in .vue file always keep in these order script -> template -> style
- Always use the `useVxeTable` composable for all table/grid implementations. Do not use Element Plus or other table components directly; wrap all table logic and configuration in `useVxeTable` for consistency, maintainability, and access to advanced features.

---

## Backend API
- All available backend APIs are defined in `/libraries/api/src/index.ts`.
- Centralize API calls here for consistency and maintainability.

---

## Forms
- Use Element Plus form components for all forms unless using FormRenderer.
- Always set label position to top in ElForm /el-form.
- If using FormRenderer, refer to [vform3 documentation](mdc:https:/www.yuque.com/visualdev/vform3).

---

## Code Style
- Use 2 spaces for indentation.
- Use camelCase for variable and function names.
- Use PascalCase for component and class names.
- Use kebab-case for file names.

### Key Points:
1. Use ResponsiveFilter component for all table filtering
2. Initialize filters in onMounted hook
3. Handle filter changes through form-change event
4. Use proper filter configuration with labels, keys, and options
5. Support both single and multiple select filters
6. Include search functionality with inputKey

### Benefits:
- Consistent UI/UX across the application
- Responsive design that adapts to available space
- Built-in support for multiple filter types
- Automatic handling of filter overflow
- Integrated search functionality
